---
// The close-the-loop story (walkthrough v2): every figure below is a capture
// from ONE order in the demo kitchen — the PO that was actually emailed, the
// receiving that recorded what the truck really brought, the price the paid
// amount became, and the shelf it landed on. Figures regenerate from the app
// repo's `npm run demo:capture` (demo/README.md there); re-check the alt/
// caption figures against fresh captures before swapping files.
const sources = ['Post supplier receiving', 'Import and review an invoice', 'Enter a purchase manually'];
---

<section id="purchases" class="container-page py-section" aria-labelledby="purchases-heading">
	<div class="grid items-start gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[5fr_6fr] lg:gap-16">
		<div data-reveal>
			<h2 id="purchases-heading" class="text-h2 font-semibold">Send the order. Receive the truck. The price you paid becomes the price you plan with.</h2>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-lede text-ink-soft">
				Confirming an event turns its shopping list into purchase orders: emailed to the
				vendors that take email, printed for the cash-and-carry stop. Receiving records
				what actually arrived, and the paid price can become tomorrow&rsquo;s current cost.
			</p>
			<ul role="list" class="mt-7 space-y-3 text-ink-soft">
				{
					sources.map((source) => (
						<li class="flex items-center gap-3">
							<span class="size-1.5 shrink-0 rotate-45 bg-amber-deep" aria-hidden="true" />
							<span>{source}</span>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>
			<div class="mt-7 border-t border-hairline pt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				<p><strong class="text-ink">You confirm the write.</strong> Receiving decisions are reviewed and posted once; imported matches remain staged until approved.</p>
				<p class="mt-3"><strong class="text-ink">A price sheet is not a purchase.</strong> It can update catalog cost without creating actual spend.</p>
			</div>

			<figure class="mt-8 max-w-xs" data-reveal>
				<img
					src="/proof/v2-price-provenance.png"
					alt="An ingredient's live cost panel: $29.82 per 3 lb pack, $21.91 per kilogram usable cost, and a saved price source reading recorded purchase, Baldor, with the receiving it came from."
					width="672"
					height="1490"
					loading="lazy"
					decoding="async"
					class="h-auto w-full bg-paper shadow-ticket"
				/>
				<figcaption class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-soft">
					The paid price becomes the live cost, with its source on record.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</div>

		<div class="flex flex-col gap-10" data-reveal>
			<figure>
				<img
					src="/proof/v2-po-email.png"
					alt="An emailed purchase order, PO-00783-002 from Maple &amp; Main Catering to Green Valley Produce: five items in whole packs, a $516.08 subtotal, payment terms, and delivery instructions."
					width="1856"
					height="1136"
					loading="lazy"
					decoding="async"
					class="h-auto w-full bg-paper shadow-ticket"
				/>
				<figcaption class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-soft">
					The email the vendor actually receives: whole packs, a subtotal, your terms, sent
					straight from the confirmed order.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<figure>
				<picture>
					<source media="(max-width: 639px)" srcset="/proof/v2-receiving-mobile.png" />
					<img
						src="/proof/v2-receiving.png"
						alt="A receiving checklist for one vendor: arugula marked short with one case saved for follow-up, baby spinach received with an extra case at $477.16 against a $410.40 estimate, and lines posted to inventory."
						width="2044"
						height="1174"
						loading="lazy"
						decoding="async"
						class="h-auto w-full bg-paper shadow-ticket"
					/>
				</picture>
				<figcaption class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-soft">
					The truck is never perfect. A short case is saved for follow-up; an invoice over the
					quote sits beside the number you were promised.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</div>
	</div>

	<figure class="mt-12" data-reveal>
		<img
			src="/proof/v2-inventory.png"
			alt="The CostCook inventory screen after receiving: shelf value and count summaries, and the received ingredient's row showing its on-hand quantity, counted today."
			width="2880"
			height="1800"
			loading="lazy"
			decoding="async"
			class="h-auto w-full bg-paper shadow-ticket"
		/>
		<figcaption class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-soft">
			And the shelf agrees: what arrived is on hand, counted today, valued at what you paid.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
</section>
